 {
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "body": [
   "A rural Michigan high school refused to let its homecoming game die. When the visiting team pulled out days before Friday night, Shepherd High School replaced its canceled varsity football game with a flag-football showdown pitting the Shepherd Bluejays against their fathers and father figures.",
   "Standish-Sterling informed Shepherd late Monday, Oct. 5, that it might not be able to field a team because of injuries and illness, then formally canceled on Wednesday, Oct. 7 \u2014 the first homecoming football cancellation in Shepherd history, according to interim varsity coach Owen Russell.",
   "## An idea from senior week",
   "The fallback plan came from Russell himself. He had organized a similar two-hand-touch game between players and dads during senior week the previous year, and when Wednesday's cancellation became official, students and the student council approved turning the idea into the night's main event. Kickoff was set for 6:30 p.m. Friday, Oct. 9.",
   "## Homecoming went on",
   "The school made sure the rest of homecoming carried on as tradition demands: the band, cheerleaders, pom team and homecoming court all took part. There was no trophy on the line \u2014 just bragging rights between the Bluejays and the dads.",
   "The story traveled nationally, with CBS Evening News featuring the improvised matchup \u2014 a reminder that in small-town football, the game sometimes matters less than the people who show up for it."
  ],
