 {
  "id": "art-0085-imf-uk-growth-forecast-2026",
  "slug": "imf-upgrades-uk-growth-2026-2027-energy-rates-warning",
  "headline": "IMF Upgrades UK Growth Forecast but Warns Energy Prices and Rates Will Bite in 2027",
  "socialHeadline": "IMF lifts UK 2026 growth to 1.3% - joint second-fastest in the G7 - but cuts 2027 and warns of energy and borrowing-cost pain",
  "summary": "The International Monetary Fund has upgraded its UK growth forecast for 2026 to 1.3% while trimming 2027 to 1.2%, warning that higher energy prices and long-term interest rates will weigh on households and investment - just weeks before Chancellor John Healey's first budget.",
  "body": [
   "The International Monetary Fund raised its UK economic growth forecast for 2026 to 1.3% in its October World Economic Outlook, up from 1.0% in July and 0.8% in April - making Britain, alongside Germany, the joint second-fastest growing economy in the G7.",
   "But the Fund trimmed its 2027 projection to 1.2% from 1.3%, warning that \"higher energy prices and long-term interest rates (will) weigh on consumption and investment.\"",
   "## Inflation still above target",
   "The IMF expects UK inflation to average 3.3% this year and 2.8% in 2027 - both above the Bank of England's 2% target - with the Bank itself projecting inflation above 4% early next year as the energy price cap rises in January. Unemployment is forecast to average 5.3%.",
   "## Budget looms",
   "The outlook lands two weeks before Chancellor John Healey's first budget on October 28, and after UK long-term borrowing costs hit their highest level since 1998 last week, according to Reuters and BBC reporting on the Fund's briefing in Bangkok. The upgrade hands Healey a stronger headline number, but the 2027 warning leaves little room for giveaways."
  ],
  "category": "Business",
  "tags": [
   "IMF",
   "UK Economy",
   "GDP",
   "Inflation",
   "Interest Rates"
  ],
