  "body": [
   "Apple made its biggest smart-home push in years on Tuesday, October 13, 2026, unveiling four products under a \"Welcome home\" Apple Experience in New York. The announcements arrived as a set of Apple Newsroom press releases rather than a keynote.",
   "## Apple HomeView",
   "The headline product is Apple HomeView, the company's first dedicated smart display: a 7-inch Liquid Retina screen on a domed speaker base with an A18 chip. It comes in $299 tabletop and $299 wall-mounted versions, with preorders starting October 13 and shipping on November 13. It supports face recognition for up to six household members, works as a home hub, shows up to nine camera feeds, and runs Siri AI in beta at launch.",
   "## HomePod mini 2 and Apple TV 4K",
   "The second-generation HomePod mini costs $149, up from the original's $99 in 2020, swapping the S5 chip for an S10, adding pink, green, blue, white and midnight colors plus a Sound Glow top light. It can pair four speakers for surround sound with the new Apple TV 4K and double as a Mac speaker; Siri AI arrives through a future software update with no date given. Preorders began October 13, with shipping on October 30.",
   "The fourth-generation Apple TV 4K jumps to $299 from $129, with an A19 chip, Wi-Fi 7, three far-field microphones for hands-free Siri, and 128GB of storage with Ethernet and Thread. It ships November 13, and a Siri AI beta arrives in tvOS 27.2, also for second-generation and later models.",
   "## Home Collection accessories",
   "Apple also introduced a Home Collection of partner-built accessories from LG Electronics and Schneider Electric: a video doorbell, an ultra-wideband smart deadbolt, two thermostats, room and temperature sensors, and indoor and outdoor cameras, starting at $39.95. They go on sale from December, with some models in early 2027, in the US and Canada \u2014 cameras also in the UK.",
   "Apple said Siri AI features lean on a paired Apple Intelligence iPhone or iPad, are not available in the EU, and that server-side features carry daily usage limits with paid expanded access planned later. \"Our new lineup makes the home more helpful, more personal, and more delightful,\" said Kaiann Drance, Apple's vice president of worldwide home product marketing."
  ],
  "breaking": false,
  "category": "Technology",
  "editorPick": false,
