 {
  "body": [
   "India has hit pause on its first merchant fee for UPI payments. The National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI) said Tuesday, October 13, 2026, that the planned 0.4% merchant discount rate on select person-to-merchant UPI transactions above 2,000 rupees (about $21) \u2014 due to take effect October 15 \u2014 will now be implemented after the festive season, on a date to be notified separately. Diwali falls on November 8 this year.",
   "## Why it was delayed",
   "NPCI cited the festive shopping season for the deferral. The fee had drawn fierce opposition from retailers, including the Confederation of All India Traders and the All India Mobile Retailers Association, as well as petrol pump operators, who warned some merchants could stop accepting UPI. A proposed \"No UPI Day\" protest was withdrawn after a September 30 meeting with Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman.",
   "## Who pays \u2014 and who doesn't",
   "The fee applies only to select person-to-merchant transactions. Person-to-person transfers, payments under 2,000 rupees and small merchants processing under 100,000 rupees a month in UPI volume are exempt. Consumers pay nothing, and the fee is capped at 300 rupees per transaction.",
   "## End of the zero-fee era",
   "The rollout would end more than six years of zero-cost UPI, after merchant fees were scrapped in January 2020. UPI processed 24.07 billion transactions worth 29.37 trillion rupees in September, with PhonePe and Google Pay accounting for roughly 80% by value, according to Reuters."
  ],
