 {
  "id": "art-0095-hungary-repeals-anti-lgbtq-law-2026",
  "slug": "hungary-repeals-orban-era-anti-lgbtq-provisions-child-protection-law",
  "headline": "Hungary's Parliament Votes to Scrap Anti-LGBTQ Rules From Child Protection Law",
  "socialHeadline": "139-51: Hungary's parliament just removed the anti-LGBTQ provisions from its 2021 child-protection law",
  "summary": "Hungary's National Assembly voted 139-51 on October 13, 2026 to remove anti-LGBTQ provisions from the 2021 Act LXXIX child-protection law, replacing them with a general safeguard for minors; the Tisza government proposed the change and Fidesz opposed it.",
  "category": "world",
  "tags": [
   "Hungary",
   "LGBTQ",
   "Parliament",
   "European Union",
   "Child Protection"
  ],
  "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T21:04:09Z",
  "status": "PUBLISHED",
  "breaking": false,
  "editorPick": false,
  "pinned": false,
  "image": {
   "src": "/images/media-generation-nv-hungary-parliament-vote-ill-0-6c89e0b0-78dc-45bc-83a9-3e3c7ef780a2.webp",
   "alt": "Flat infographic illustration of a parliament dome, scales of justice and a ballot box with a checkmark representing a legislative vote",
   "caption": "Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.",
   "credit": "NewsVeria generated illustration"
  },
  "body": [
   "Hungary's National Assembly voted 139-51 on Tuesday to remove anti-LGBTQ provisions from the country's 2021 child-protection law, one of the most contested statutes of the Viktor Orban era.",
   "## What the vote changed",
   "Act LXXIX of 2021 had banned the portrayal or promotion of homosexuality and gender diversity to anyone under 18, drawing condemnation from Brussels and a ruling against the law from the European Court of Justice in April 2026. The amendment strips out those specific bans and replaces them with a general rule protecting under-18s from material that seriously impairs their development.",
   "## The politics behind it",
   "The change was proposed by the government of Prime Minister Peter Magyar's Tisza party, which holds 141 of the assembly's 199 seats, and was opposed by Fidesz, the party that enacted the original law. The vote follows years of pressure from the European Union over the statute's compatibility with European law.",
   "## What remains",
   "Rights advocates welcomed the repeal but cautioned that other discriminatory provisions remain on the books in Hungarian law, and said they would watch how the new general minor-protection standard is applied in practice."
  ],
  "sources": [
   {
    "name": "AFP reporting on the Hungarian National Assembly vote",
    "role": "primary",
    "url": "https://www.deutschetageszeitung.de/en/Politics/923653-hungary-cuts-anti-lgbtq-measures-from-orban-era-law.html"
   },
   {
    "name": "Deutsche Welle",
    "role": "corroboration",
    "url": "https://scoopfeeds.com/article/0ab428f1-abcc-5324-adea-2fdc616006f8"
   }
  ],
  "verification": {
   "eventConfirmed": true,
   "primarySourceFound": true,
   "independentCorroboration": true,
   "chronologyChecked": true,
   "detailsChecked": true,
   "imageChecked": true,
   "checkedAt": "2026-10-13T21:04:09Z",
   "notes": "Vote tally 139-51 and date 2026-10-13 consistent across AFP, DW and GCN. Excluded an EU-funds-unlock figure found only in secondary outlets. HRW caveat on remaining discriminatory provisions included."
  }
 },
