 {
  "id": "art-0098-openai-anthropic-uk-ai-regulation-2026",
  "slug": "openai-anthropic-uk-lawmakers-ai-regulation-2026",
  "headline": "OpenAI and Anthropic Tell UK Lawmakers Voluntary AI Safety Pledges Are Not Enough",
  "socialHeadline": "OpenAI and Anthropic just told UK lawmakers: make AI safety rules binding \u2014 voluntary pledges aren't enough",
  "summary": "Executives from OpenAI and Anthropic told a UK parliamentary committee on October 13, 2026 that voluntary AI safety pledges are no longer enough and backed binding statutory rules, while Google DeepMind and Meta declined to support new legislation.",
  "category": "tech",
  "tags": [
   "Artificial Intelligence",
   "OpenAI",
   "Anthropic",
   "United Kingdom",
   "AI Regulation"
  ],
  "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T21:06:05Z",
  "status": "PUBLISHED",
  "breaking": false,
  "editorPick": false,
  "pinned": false,
  "image": {
   "src": "/images/media-generation-nv-ai-regulation-uk-parliament-0-7571d529-79e5-4c4a-bc5a-04aaf37dba98.webp",
   "alt": "Flat infographic illustration of a parliament building beside a robot head with circuit patterns and a gavel",
   "caption": "Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.",
   "credit": "NewsVeria generated illustration"
  },
  "body": [
   "Executives from OpenAI and Anthropic told British lawmakers on Tuesday that voluntary safety pledges are no longer enough for artificial intelligence and that binding statutory rules are needed.",
   "## What they said",
   "Appearing before the House of Commons Business, Innovation, Science and Trade Committee's AI security session, Anthropic's Liam Booth-Smith said 'the rules should be binding.' OpenAI's Tom Duff Gordon, the company's head of EMEA policy, backed mandatory incident reporting and a statutory framework. Both companies also promised to halt a model if MI5, MI6, GCHQ or the AI Security Institute showed it was unsafe. Google DeepMind and Meta declined to support new legislation.",
   "## The security backdrop",
   "The session took place alongside a warning from MI5 chief Sir Ken McCallum that AI is 'supercharging' the security threats facing the United Kingdom, sharpening the debate over whether the country's largely voluntary approach to AI oversight needs teeth.",
   "The committee's inquiry continues; any legislation would follow the government's response to its findings."
  ],
  "sources": [
   {
    "name": "WIRED \u2014 OpenAI and Anthropic tell British lawmakers: regulate us",
    "role": "primary",
    "url": "https://www.wired.com/story/openai-and-anthropic-tell-british-lawmakers-regulate-us/"
   },
   {
    "name": "Sky News (via Capital FM) \u2014 Tech firms promise to take down AI platforms",
    "role": "corroboration",
    "url": "https://www.capitalfm.com/radio/news/uk-world/tech-firms-promise-to-take-down-ai-platforms/"
   },
   {
    "name": "UK Parliament \u2014 Business, Innovation, Science and Trade Committee",
    "role": "corroboration",
    "url": "https://committees.parliament.uk/committee/365/business-innovation-science-and-trade-committee/news/217956/"
   }
  ],
  "verification": {
   "eventConfirmed": true,
   "primarySourceFound": true,
   "independentCorroboration": true,
   "chronologyChecked": true,
   "detailsChecked": true,
   "imageChecked": true,
   "checkedAt": "2026-10-13T21:06:05Z",
   "notes": "WIRED page fetch-blocked in this environment; quotes cross-checked via Sky News reporting and the official Parliament record. Executive name rendered 'Liam Booth-Smith' per committee record."
  }
